Relational analysis

surmounts [scope: shrine] 
Avalokiteśvara is elevated on a high platform within the inner hall
Child deity figures stand at floor level in the forecourt. 
Vertical separation here operates across the full depth of the shrine. (Must look up and inward to engage Avalokiteshvara)
Elevation encodes the principal deity's position at the apex of the sacred hierarchy operative within this space.

brackets [scope: shrine] 
Two child deity figures function as a paired unit framing the entrance to the inner hall. 
Each is the other's counterpart, together constituting the iconographic marking of the threshold. 
The red doorframe they stand beside amplifies this bracketing function architecturally.

admits [scope: shrine] 
They mediate the devotee's entry. 
The admits relation encodes the gateway function: 
the devotee's passage from forecourt to inner hall is iconographically authorized and mediated by these figures. 
Their presence transforms an architectural doorway into a ritually constituted threshold.

subordinates 
Position-independent rank statement. 
Their hierarchical relation to Avalokiteśvara is stable — they serve under her soteriological authority.

coordinates[depth, grouped, permanent] [scope: shrine] 
The depth-axis coordination is the geometric description of what is otherwise described relationally above. 
The two child deity figures and the principal deity are arranged along the depth axis running from the entrance toward the rear wall. 
Grouping is deliberate and permanent.

Interpretation
Three of the five interpretation rules directly engaged.

R1: Child deities are visually subordinate — smaller in scale, standing on modest pedestals, placed outside the main hall.
Avalokiteśvara is dominant in scale and occupies the most elevated and spatially central position. 
A scale-and-position reading would assign all authority to the central deity and treat the threshold figures as minor. 
But within their own functional register — apotropaic boundary management — the child deities are the operative agents. 

R2: The status of each figure is determined by its function within the ritual context of the shrine. 
Avalokiteśvara's soteriological and intercessory authority is supreme within the devotional programme. 
Child deities' apotropaic authority is operative at the threshold. 
These are functionally differentiated roles within the same ensemble, each carrying the status appropriate to its domain.

R4: each child deity simultaneously brackets the threshold, guards the interior, and admits the devotee. 
All three relations are concurrently true and none cancels the others.

