The two multi-tiered pagodas stand on the altar platform immediately flanking the central deity, one to each side. They are architectural-iconographic objects rather than figurative icons, but they function iconographically: the pagoda in Chinese Buddhist tradition is a reliquary structure that embeds the presence of the Buddha and the cosmos within a single vertical form — its tiers encode cosmological levels, and its presence on an altar consecrates the space as a microcosm of the Buddhist universe. Their paired placement flanking Avalokiteśvara encodes a cosmological framing of the central deity's sacred space. 

ICON₆: Maitreya
POSITION    : axial, recessed
SCALE       : subordinate
ORIENTATION : front-facing
FUNCTION    : soteriological, cosmological, devotional

Maitreya's placement on the central axis immediately before the main altar is a compressed theological statement about the relationship between present compassionate activity and future soteriological fulfillment. The small scale encodes eschatological anticipation rather than hierarchical subordination. The recessed axial position encodes the condition of the not-yet-manifest rather than marginal status. And the transmits relation connecting Avalokiteśvara to Maitreya encodes the ensemble's deepest soteriological claim: that the compassion presently at work in this hall is the very activity through which the conditions for the next Buddha's arrival are being cultivated. The grammar describes his position; the identification reveals that he is, quietly and precisely, the ensemble's eschatological horizon. 

The mandorla as a FRAMES relation
The radiating mandorla surrounding Avalokiteśvara — the vast halo of arms, heads, and golden light emanating from the central figure — requires special treatment. It is not a separate ICON but an iconographic attribute of the principal deity that simultaneously functions as a spatial boundary. In RFIG terms it generates a frames relation that is internal to ICON₁:
[MANDORLA  frames  AVALOKITEŚVARA]  [scope: altar]
This is iconographically significant because the mandorla transforms the central figure into a self-enclosed sacred cosmos. The arms radiate outward in all directions, reaching toward every zone of the hall — upward, sideways, forward — while the central consciousness remains still in meditation. The mandorla therefore encodes both the boundlessness of Avalokiteśvara's compassionate reach and the bounded identity of the sacred figure at its centre. No other icon in the hall has this self-framing quality.

