Relational analysis

centers [scope: shrine] Every element in the hall is organized spatially and hierarchically around Avalokiteśvara. The pagodas flank at altar level; the arhats flank at wall level; the devotional furniture fills the nave along the approach axis. The centers relation is not simply a description of visual prominence — it encodes the fact that the principal deity is the organizing principle of the entire spatial programme. Remove it and the ensemble has no coherent structure; everything else becomes a collection of unrelated objects.

mirrors — pagoda pair [scope: altar] The two pagodas are bilaterally symmetric at the altar level, equal in scale, identical in form, positioned equidistant from the central axis. Their mirroring is not merely decorative — it encodes the cosmological claim that the Buddhist universe is ordered and balanced, with the principal deity at its axis. The pagodas together function as a paired cosmological frame for the central figure at altar scope.

mirrors — arhat pair [scope: shrine] The two arhat groups mirror each other at shrine scope: nine figures on the left, nine on the right, in comparable poses and arrangements. Unlike the pagoda pair — whose mirroring is exact — the arhat groups mirror at the level of ensemble structure rather than individual figure. Each group is internally varied (each arhat has a distinct iconographic identity) while the two groups are bilaterally equivalent as wholes. This is a higher-order mirroring: symmetric structure containing internal differentiation.
faces — arhat groups [scope: shrine] The consistent inward-facing orientation of the arhat figures is one of the most analytically significant features of this ensemble. The arhats do not face the devotee; they face the central deity and the nave between them. This orientation encodes their position as witnesses and companions within the sacred assembly rather than as figures engaging the devotee directly. The devotee who enters the hall is not the primary addressee of the arhats' gaze — the principal deity is. The devotee enters a space in which an already-constituted assembly of enlightened beings is in permanent orientation toward the central sacred presence.

surmounts [scope: shrine] Avalokiteśvara's elevation above both the pagodas and the arhat groups encodes hierarchical rank through vertical position. The central figure is not simply taller — it is enthroned on a raised platform that places it in a distinct vertical register from everything else in the hall. The arhats at wall level and the pagodas at altar level both operate in lower registers, with Avalokiteśvara commanding the highest point of the iconographic programme.

frames [scope: altar] The mandorla's role deserves extended treatment. In standard iconographic description, a mandorla is simply a halo or glory — a decorative attribute. In RFIG terms it generates a genuine spatial relation: the radiating arms create a visual boundary around the central figure that defines its sacred space as distinct from the surrounding hall. The mandorla of the thousand-armed form is unusually powerful in this respect because the arms themselves extend into the hall's space — they are not a flat painted halo but a three-dimensional radiating structure that actively reaches outward while the central figure remains still. This creates a paradox that is itself iconographically meaningful: the deity is simultaneously bounded (framed, centered, enthroned) and unbounded (reaching into every register of space with compassionate action).

Analysis
I. The spatial axis as axis mundi
Eliade's concept of the axis mundi — the cosmic pillar or world axis that connects the three registers of heaven, earth, and underworld, and around which sacred space is organized — finds direct material expression in the spatial programme of this hall. The central axis running from the entrance threshold through the incense censer and the nave to the enthroned Avalokiteśvara is not merely an architectural convenience; it is a cosmological claim. The principal deity is positioned at the point where this axis terminates — or rather, where it originates, since for Eliade the sacred centre is always the source from which ordered space radiates outward.
In RFIG terms, the centers relation encodes this geometrically. From a History of Religions perspective, what the grammar describes as compositional centrality is simultaneously a hierophantic claim: this point in space is qualitatively different from all surrounding space because the sacred has manifested here. The entire spatial organization of the hall — the bilateral symmetry of the arhat groups, the paired pagodas, the rows of devotional furniture along the approach axis — is a consequence of and response to this primary hierophany.
The two flanking pagodas deepen this reading. The pagoda form in Chinese Buddhist tradition derives from the Indian stūpa, itself understood as an architectural axis mundi — a structure that replicates the cosmic mountain at the centre of the Buddhist universe, Mount Meru, and embeds within it the Buddha's presence in the form of relics. Their placement flanking Avalokiteśvara does not merely frame the central deity decoratively; it surrounds her with two additional instantiations of the cosmic axis, creating a concentrated axis mundi complex at the altar's centre. The RFIG mirrors and coordinates relations between the pagodas describe their geometric relationship; the History of Religions reading explains why that geometric relationship carries sacred weight.

II. The threshold as liminal zone
Van Gennep's analysis of rites of passage identified the threshold — the limen — as a zone of structural ambiguity: the person or object at the threshold belongs fully neither to the space being left nor to the space being entered. Turner extended this into a broader theory of liminality as a condition of heightened sacred potential, in which normal social and spatial categories are suspended and encounter with transformative power becomes possible.
The forecourt-to-inner-hall transition documented in the first photograph — the armored guardian pair, the incense censer on the central axis, the red doorframe — is a classically constituted liminal zone in van Gennep's sense. The guardians at the threshold do not simply mark a spatial boundary; they mark a categorical boundary between profane and sacred space. The RFIG grammar captures their functional roles through brackets, guards, and admits. The History of Religions reading identifies what these functional roles are for: they manage the transition across a boundary that is not merely spatial but ontological — a boundary between the ordinary world and the hierophantic space where the sacred has made itself present.
The incense censer positioned on the central axis immediately inside this threshold is particularly significant from this perspective. Incense in Chinese Buddhist practice is not simply an offering — it is a medium of communication across the boundary between the human and divine registers. Its placement at the liminal zone, on the axial line connecting the threshold to the principal deity, encodes the moment at which the devotee's communication with the sacred begins — not yet in the full presence of the deity, but already oriented toward it across the transitional space.

III. The numinous and the aesthetic of overwhelming presence
Rudolf Otto's phenomenology of the numinous — the experience of the mysterium tremendum et fascinans, the holy as simultaneously terrifying and compelling, utterly other and yet drawing the experiencer toward it — finds unusually direct material expression in the thousand-armed form of Avalokiteśvara.
The iconographic programme of the thousand-armed form is designed to overwhelm. The multiple arms radiating in all directions, the additional faces rising in tiers above the primary face, the sheer physical scale of the statue relative to any human body standing before it — all of these are aesthetic strategies for inducing the specific quality of experience Otto describes: the sense of confronting something that exceeds the human scale entirely, that is simultaneously comprehensible (a face, hands, a seated body) and incomprehensible (a thousand arms, eleven faces, a body that fills the entire rear wall of the hall). Otto called this quality augustum — the awe-inducing majesty of the sacred that produces in the devotee not merely admiration but a form of ontological smallness.
The RFIG grammar records the mandorla as a frames relation — the radiating arms create a spatial boundary around the central figure. The History of Religions reading asks what experience this framing is designed to produce: the devotee who enters the hall and confronts this form is positioned as a finite being before an infinite compassion. The mandorla does not merely contain the deity; it performs the deity's boundlessness. The arms reach into every register of space — upward, sideways, forward into the nave — while the central consciousness remains perfectly still in meditation. This is an iconographic statement about the nature of Avalokiteśvara's compassion: inexhaustible, omnidirectional, reaching into every zone of suffering, yet rooted in an unmoving centre of awakened awareness.

IV. Sacred assembly and communitas
The Eighteen Arhats lining the lateral walls introduce a dimension that the purely grammatical analysis captures structurally — their inward-facing orientation, their lateral mirroring, their subordinate scale — but that requires a History of Religions reading to interpret fully.
Turner's concept of communitas describes the condition of deep, undifferentiated community that emerges within liminal space, in which ordinary social hierarchies are dissolved and participants are bound together by shared encounter with the sacred. The arhat assembly encodes something structurally analogous. The Eighteen Arhats have individually transcended the conditions of ordinary existence — they have attained liberation, they are no longer subject to rebirth — and yet they remain in the world, oriented together toward the Dharma and its principal embodiment. Their inward-facing orientation is the iconographic statement of this shared orientation: they are not facing the devotee, not facing the exterior world, but facing inward toward the sacred centre they collectively attend.
When a devotee enters this hall, they enter a space in which this assembly is already in place. The devotional furniture — the rows of low tables and kneeling cushions arranged in the nave — literally positions the human worshipper within the assembly, extending the rows of seated figures from the walls into the nave itself. From a History of Religions perspective, this is not merely a spatial arrangement but a soteriological claim: the devotee who takes their place in the nave joins, from the human end, a community that extends from the living congregation through the historical assembly of arhats to the transcendent compassion of Avalokiteśvara at its centre. The spatial programme of the hall encodes the structure of the Buddhist path — the human practitioner, the exemplary community of liberated beings, and the bodhisattva whose compassion makes liberation possible.

V. Hierophany and the cosmogram
Eliade's concept of the imago mundi — the sacred space as a replica of the cosmos, so that to be within the temple is to be at the centre of the world — finds its fullest expression in the complete ensemble read as a whole.
The vertical axis encodes cosmological hierarchy: Avalokiteśvara at the apex, the arhat assembly in the middle register, the devotee at the base. The horizontal axes encode cosmological order: perfect bilateral symmetry on both the lateral walls and the altar platform, with the central axis as the principle of organization. The pagodas embed the cosmological mountain within the altar space. The mandorla radiates the deity's presence into all directions of space simultaneously. The threshold guardians mark the boundary between the ordered cosmos within and the undifferentiated space without.
What RFIG describes grammatically as a set of nested relations — centers, surmounts, mirrors, frames, brackets — is, from a History of Religions perspective, a cosmogram: a spatial representation of the structure of reality as understood within the Mahayana Buddhist worldview. To enter this hall and take one's place in the nave is not merely to visit a religious building; it is to locate oneself within a materially instantiated model of the cosmos, oriented toward its compassionate centre, in the company of those who have already completed the journey the devotee is undertaking.

Compound-level context: the Mahavira Hall
The Mahavira Hall (大雄寶殿) is the monastery's principal hall, housing the Three Buddhas — canonically Śākyamuni flanked by Amitābha and Bhaiṣajyaguru, or in some configurations the Three Bodies of the Buddha. It is the soteriological and cosmological centre of the entire compound. All compound-scope relations in this analysis are directed toward or organized around this hall as the principal sacred node.
