Śākyamuni is enthroned on the central lotus platform, his hands in dhyāna mudrā — the meditation gesture, palms resting in the lap — encoding perfect stillness and awakened awareness. His mandorla is the largest in the hall, filling the full height of the altar space behind him, and carries at its apex a small seated Buddha figure — a miniature icon embedded within the larger mandorla, encoding the inexhaustible proliferation of Buddhahood across all directions of space and time. The central position, dominant scale, and axial placement make him the unambiguous hierarchical and compositional anchor of the Three Buddhas ensemble. 
ICON₂ — Left Buddha (Amitābha, 阿彌陀佛)
POSITION    : peripheral, elevated, paired-lateral
SCALE       : equal (relative to right Buddha),
              subordinate (relative to Śākyamuni)
ORIENTATION : front-facing (toward main entrance)
FUNCTION    : soteriological, devotional
ICON₃ — Right Buddha (Bhaiṣajyaguru, 藥師佛)
POSITION    : peripheral, elevated, paired-lateral
SCALE       : equal (relative to left Buddha),
              subordinate (relative to Śākyamuni)
ORIENTATION : front-facing (toward main entrance)
FUNCTION    : soteriological, devotional
The two flanking Buddhas are iconographically distinct from Śākyamuni but equal to each other in scale, position, and compositional weight. Their mandorlas, though slightly smaller than Śākyamuni's, are of the same form and golden register, creating a unified visual programme across the three figures. Identification of the left figure as Amitābha and the right as Bhaiṣajyaguru follows the canonical arrangement of the 横三世佛 (Three Buddhas of the Three Directions) — Śākyamuni presiding over the present world, Amitābha over the Western Pure Land, Bhaiṣajyaguru over the Eastern Lapis Lazuli realm — though confirmation against the hall's own textual identifications would be required for fieldwork documentation. 
ICON₄ — Left Attendant (Ānanda, 阿難)
POSITION    : axial, paired-lateral
SCALE       : subordinate
ORIENTATION : inward-facing (toward Śākyamuni)
FUNCTION    : pedagogical, devotional
ICON₅ — Right Attendant (Mahākāśyapa, 迦葉)
POSITION    : axial, paired-lateral
SCALE       : subordinate
ORIENTATION : inward-facing (toward Śākyamuni)
FUNCTION    : pedagogical, devotional
The two white-robed standing figures flanking Śākyamuni are his canonical paired disciples in Chinese Buddhist iconography. Ānanda — the younger figure, associated with memory and the transmission of the sūtras — and Mahākāśyapa — the elder, associated with meditative discipline and the transmission of mind-to-mind awakening — constitute the paradigm pedagogical pair in Chinese Buddhist iconographic tradition. Their inward-facing orientation encodes their permanent attentiveness to the teacher they serve: unlike the apotropaic figures elsewhere in this corpus who face outward toward threat, these figures face inward toward the source of Dharma they have heard, remembered, and transmitted. Their white robes and simplified forms distinguish them visually from the golden Buddhas, marking the ontological distinction between a fully awakened Buddha and his enlightened but not yet fully Buddhahood-attained disciples. 
ICON₆ — Avalokiteśvara (觀世音菩薩) (Rear of hall, facing back entrance)
POSITION    : axial, elevated, recessed
SCALE       : dominant (altar scope, rear programme);
              subordinate (hall scope, relative to Three Buddhas)
ORIENTATION : outward-facing (toward back entrance)
FUNCTION    : soteriological, intercessory, devotional
Avalokiteśvara is enthroned at the rear of the hall on a raised platform within an architectural niche, a large flame-leaf mandorla rising behind her. She faces outward through the back entrance — the same outward-facing orientation Skanda holds at the back of the Hall of Heavenly Kings, but carrying an entirely different function. Where Skanda's outward orientation is apotropaic — directed against threat — Avalokiteśvara's outward orientation is intercessory and welcoming: she faces the monastery's rear zones and the world beyond the back threshold with compassionate attention. Her dhyāna posture encodes stillness and meditative absorption; the mandorla encodes the boundary of her sacred space.
Her position in relation to the Three Buddhas activates the opposes relation for the second time in this compound analysis, but here the two poles of the opposition encode a specifically Buddhist theological claim rather than a threshold-management programme.
